1
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:13,713
Basel is a small city. Two hundred thousand people,
on a bend in the Rhine where three

2
00:00:13,733 --> 00:00:20,061
countries meet, with a cathedral, a fifteenth-century
university, and a carnival in February

3
00:00:20,081 --> 00:00:25,926
that the people who live there take more seriously
than anything else in their lives.

4
00:00:26,496 --> 00:00:32,063
It is also, and has been for a century and a half,
the capital of the world's medicines.

5
00:00:32,083 --> 00:00:37,650
The great houses grew up there in the
eighteen-hundreds — dye works that discovered that

6
00:00:37,670 --> 00:00:43,236
the chemistry of colour and the chemistry of the body
are cousins — and they never left.

7
00:00:43,256 --> 00:00:48,823
By twenty forty-two, four of the six largest
pharmaceutical companies on Earth had their

8
00:00:48,843 --> 00:00:52,758
headquarters within five kilometres of
that bend in the river.

9
00:00:53,329 --> 00:00:55,036
Three of them were Umbrella.

10
00:00:57,106 --> 00:01:03,585
Halvorsen Pike had brought the first, in twenty
thirty-two. The second came in thirty-eight,

11
00:01:03,605 --> 00:01:09,730
and the third in forty-one, and neither was a
hostile takeover; by then, being acquired

12
00:01:09,750 --> 00:01:15,945
by Umbrella was less like being conquered than like
being adopted by a wealthier family.

13
00:01:16,516 --> 00:01:22,824
By twenty forty-two, Umbrella employed thirty-one
thousand people in the canton of Basel-Stadt,

14
00:01:22,844 --> 00:01:29,218
which had a working population of a hundred and twenty
thousand. It paid, directly, thirty-eight

15
00:01:29,238 --> 00:01:35,147
percent of the canton's corporate tax. Its foundation
had rebuilt the university hospital

16
00:01:35,167 --> 00:01:40,876
and endowed nineteen chairs. It had bought the
football club, which was how you really

17
00:01:40,896 --> 00:01:44,673
take a Swiss city, and the club had
won the league twice.

18
00:01:46,742 --> 00:01:52,183
The Basel Compact was eighteen pages long and
did not mention sovereignty once.

19
00:01:52,754 --> 00:01:59,244
It was an agreement between Umbrella Life Sciences and
the canton of Basel-Stadt, negotiated

20
00:01:59,264 --> 00:02:05,684
over two years, and it was, on its face, a perfectly
ordinary piece of Swiss administrative

21
00:02:05,704 --> 00:02:11,912
law — the sort of arrangement between a canton and a
major employer that Switzerland has

22
00:02:11,932 --> 00:02:14,813
been making since the nineteenth century.

23
00:02:15,383 --> 00:02:21,669
Umbrella undertook to invest forty billion francs
over ten years. It undertook to keep

24
00:02:21,689 --> 00:02:27,974
thirty-one thousand jobs in the canton, and to add
nine thousand. It undertook to fund

25
00:02:27,994 --> 00:02:33,326
the hospital, the university, the tram
extension, and the flood defences.

26
00:02:33,895 --> 00:02:40,256
In exchange, the canton agreed to a single joint
body — the Basel Life Sciences Council

27
00:02:40,276 --> 00:02:46,857
— which would have binding authority over licensing,
planning, research permits, biosafety

28
00:02:46,877 --> 00:02:53,677
inspection, and the training curriculum of the
university's medical and scientific faculties.

29
00:02:54,248 --> 00:03:00,515
The Council had nine members. Four were appointed by
the canton. Four were appointed by

30
00:03:00,535 --> 00:03:06,657
Umbrella. The ninth was the Rector of the
University of Basel, whose chair was funded

31
00:03:06,677 --> 00:03:08,608
by the Umbrella Foundation.

32
00:03:11,176 --> 00:03:17,162
The canton put it to a referendum, because
Switzerland puts everything to a referendum.

33
00:03:17,182 --> 00:03:19,509
The turnout was sixty-one percent.

34
00:03:20,080 --> 00:03:22,991
It passed with seventy-four
percent in favour.

35
00:03:23,562 --> 00:03:29,460
Nobody was coerced. Nobody was paid. Seventy-four
percent of the people of Basel looked

36
00:03:29,480 --> 00:03:35,583
at the hospital, and the trams, and the jobs, and the
flood defences, and their children's

37
00:03:35,603 --> 00:03:41,570
tuition, and voted, freely, to hand the oversight of
the most dangerous science on Earth

38
00:03:41,590 --> 00:03:47,353
to a council on which the people doing the science
had four seats out of nine and the

39
00:03:47,373 --> 00:03:48,578
chairman's salary.

40
00:03:50,768 --> 00:03:51,778
A vote.

41
00:03:52,300 --> 00:03:53,004
A vote.

42
00:03:53,525 --> 00:03:59,224
I took a country with a vote. They have never
forgiven me for it, and they have spent

43
00:03:59,244 --> 00:04:02,252
a hundred years pretending it
was only tanks.

44
00:04:02,771 --> 00:04:05,900
The difference is that you had to burn
the parliament first.

45
00:04:06,420 --> 00:04:09,621
Yes. You did not even need to visit.

46
00:04:12,141 --> 00:04:16,377
The people who tried to kill him had been
planning it for nineteen months.

47
00:04:16,947 --> 00:04:22,926
It was not a government. That mattered a great deal,
afterwards. It was four people in

48
00:04:22,946 --> 00:04:29,483
an apartment in Oslo: a Norwegian pharmaceutical heiress
who had lost her company; a dismissed

49
00:04:29,503 --> 00:04:35,481
American FBI agent; a former officer of the British
Secret Intelligence Service; and a

50
00:04:35,501 --> 00:04:41,689
woman from Basel named Ursula Frey, whose younger
brother had been one of the two hundred

51
00:04:41,709 --> 00:04:43,014
and sixty thousand.

52
00:04:43,584 --> 00:04:49,505
Julian Ashcombe — who had taken early retirement in
twenty forty and told his colleagues

53
00:04:49,525 --> 00:04:55,378
he intended to write a book about fly-fishing — had
spent four years building a picture

54
00:04:55,398 --> 00:05:01,183
out of money, and then four more building one out of
people, because a chief financial

55
00:05:01,203 --> 00:05:06,515
officer had told him on a satellite phone that
the money was the wrong picture.

56
00:05:07,086 --> 00:05:12,688
He had concluded three things. First, that Albert
Wesker was not a human being in any

57
00:05:12,708 --> 00:05:18,311
sense that mattered. Second, that no institution on
Earth would ever act against him,

58
00:05:18,331 --> 00:05:24,530
because every institution on Earth now depended on him —
a conclusion he had reached formally,

59
00:05:24,550 --> 00:05:30,153
in writing, in his last month of service, in a
paper that four people had read before

60
00:05:30,173 --> 00:05:35,842
it disappeared into a vault. And third, that the
only remaining option was murder, and

61
00:05:35,862 --> 00:05:41,597
that it would not work, and that it should be
attempted anyway, because the alternative

62
00:05:41,617 --> 00:05:44,442
was to do nothing for the rest
of his life.

63
00:05:46,132 --> 00:05:47,261
It won't work.

64
00:05:47,781 --> 00:05:48,625
No.

65
00:05:49,144 --> 00:05:54,002
I put fifteen rounds in him from eight feet. He
gave them back to me in my hand.

66
00:05:54,523 --> 00:05:57,056
I know, Owen. I've held one.

67
00:05:59,076 --> 00:06:00,080
Then why?

68
00:06:02,100 --> 00:06:06,523
Because in seventeen years he has never once been
surprised in public. Everything the

69
00:06:06,543 --> 00:06:11,072
world knows about him, he has arranged. If we cannot
kill him, we can at least make him

70
00:06:11,092 --> 00:06:14,680
do something in front of four hundred
cameras that he has not chosen.

71
00:06:17,199 --> 00:06:22,833
The signing was in the Münsterplatz, in front of the
cathedral, on a bright cold morning

72
00:06:22,853 --> 00:06:23,411
in March.

73
00:06:23,980 --> 00:06:29,672
There were four hundred cameras. There were nine
thousand people in the square. There

74
00:06:29,692 --> 00:06:35,853
were the canton's executive council, and the Rector,
and the mayor, and a federal councillor

75
00:06:35,873 --> 00:06:41,498
from Bern, and a stage with a long table on it, and
behind the table a row of flags.

76
00:06:42,069 --> 00:06:47,921
The Director signed at ten eighteen. He stood up. He
shook the hand of the President of

77
00:06:47,941 --> 00:06:51,499
the Cantonal Executive. He turned
to face the square.

78
00:06:55,569 --> 00:07:01,300
The narrator will not tell you what was in the van,
or how it came to be there, or who

79
00:07:01,320 --> 00:07:07,052
signed for what. Those details exist in a Swiss
federal file that runs to nine hundred

80
00:07:07,072 --> 00:07:13,005
pages, and they are nobody's business now. It is
enough to say that it was parked fifteen

81
00:07:13,025 --> 00:07:17,820
metres from the stage, and that it had been
the work of nineteen months.

82
00:07:18,391 --> 00:07:24,202
It killed four people. A sound engineer named Peter
Aebi. Two members of the cantonal

83
00:07:24,222 --> 00:07:30,170
police. And Ursula Frey, who had been standing much
closer than she had told the others

84
00:07:30,190 --> 00:07:36,001
she would be, which Julian Ashcombe understood the
moment he heard the casualty list,

85
00:07:36,021 --> 00:07:38,540
and which he never spoke about again.

86
00:07:39,110 --> 00:07:44,725
It injured a hundred and nine. It took the front off
the stage, and the first four rows

87
00:07:44,745 --> 00:07:48,612
of chairs, and every window on the
north side of the square.

88
00:07:51,682 --> 00:07:54,282
The smoke took about forty
seconds to thin.

89
00:07:54,851 --> 00:08:00,811
Four hundred cameras were still running. Most of
them were on the ground, or pointed at

90
00:08:00,831 --> 00:08:07,066
the sky, or being carried by people who were running.
Twenty of them were still on tripods,

91
00:08:07,086 --> 00:08:13,252
still framed on the stage, still broadcasting live to
an audience that had by then become,

92
00:08:13,272 --> 00:08:16,826
in the space of a minute, most of
the human species.

93
00:08:17,396 --> 00:08:23,268
And as the smoke thinned, on eight live feeds at once,
the world saw a tall figure standing

94
00:08:23,288 --> 00:08:25,535
in the middle of the wrecked stage.

95
00:08:26,105 --> 00:08:31,662
He was covered in grey dust. His coat was torn at
the left shoulder. There was no blood

96
00:08:31,682 --> 00:08:32,560
on him at all.

97
00:08:33,129 --> 00:08:38,933
He reached up and brushed the dust off his shoulder.
Twice. The way you would after brushing

98
00:08:38,953 --> 00:08:39,692
past a wall.

99
00:08:40,263 --> 00:08:45,865
Then he bent down, and picked up a chair that had
been blown over, and set it upright,

100
00:08:45,885 --> 00:08:51,488
and looked out at the square — at the nine thousand
people, at the sixteen cameras, at

101
00:08:51,508 --> 00:08:57,699
the world — and said, in a voice that the microphone on
the fallen lectern picked up perfectly:

102
00:08:59,889 --> 00:09:01,148
Is everyone all right?

103
00:09:03,668 --> 00:09:09,145
He walked down off the front of the stage, which
was four feet high and no longer had

104
00:09:09,165 --> 00:09:14,836
steps, and went to a member of the cantonal police
who was lying against the base of it,

105
00:09:14,856 --> 00:09:20,010
and knelt, and put his hand on the man's chest,
and said something nobody heard.

106
00:09:20,581 --> 00:09:26,666
Then he stood, and looked around the square, and found
the President of the Cantonal Executive

107
00:09:26,686 --> 00:09:32,251
being helped up by two aides, and said — and this is
the sentence that would be quoted

108
00:09:32,271 --> 00:09:37,837
for the next thousand years, in every language, on
every world that human beings would

109
00:09:37,857 --> 00:09:38,552
ever reach:

110
00:09:40,741 --> 00:09:42,257
Shall we continue the meeting?

111
00:09:45,778 --> 00:09:48,946
It is difficult, now, to explain
what that did.

112
00:09:49,515 --> 00:09:55,428
Thirteen billion views in four days — the largest
single event in the history of human

113
00:09:55,448 --> 00:10:01,291
attention. It was watched more than the Moon
landing had been watched by everyone who

114
00:10:01,311 --> 00:10:07,775
had ever lived. Every news organisation on Earth ran the
same four seconds: the dust thinning,

115
00:10:07,795 --> 00:10:11,363
the figure standing, the hand
brushing the shoulder.

116
00:10:11,932 --> 00:10:17,513
And what people felt, overwhelmingly, was not
suspicion. That came later, from a small

117
00:10:17,533 --> 00:10:23,049
number of them, and they were shouted down. What
people felt was awe, and love, and a

118
00:10:23,069 --> 00:10:26,045
kind of relief that they could
not have named.

119
00:10:26,615 --> 00:10:32,088
Because the world of twenty forty-two was
frightened. It had been frightened for a very

120
00:10:32,108 --> 00:10:37,834
long time — of storms, and of wars, and of a disease
that had killed a quarter of a million

121
00:10:37,854 --> 00:10:43,201
people, and of the slow visible failure of every
institution that had once claimed to

122
00:10:43,221 --> 00:10:48,757
be in charge. And here, in a cathedral square, under
four hundred cameras, was a man who

123
00:10:48,777 --> 00:10:54,440
had been bombed at six metres and had stood up and
brushed the dust off his coat and asked

124
00:10:54,460 --> 00:10:56,398
whether everyone was all right.

125
00:10:56,967 --> 00:11:02,947
Nine thousand people in the Münsterplatz did not run
away. They stayed, and some of them

126
00:11:02,967 --> 00:11:08,810
wept, and some of them began, raggedly, without
anyone leading them, to say eighty-one

127
00:11:08,830 --> 00:11:09,220
words.

128
00:11:11,789 --> 00:11:13,732
Catherine Adeyemi had known.

129
00:11:14,302 --> 00:11:19,693
Not the detail. Not the date, or the van, or the
woman from the quarry office. She had

130
00:11:19,713 --> 00:11:25,481
known, from one sentence in one call, that Julian
Ashcombe had stopped gathering information

131
00:11:25,501 --> 00:11:31,143
and started planning something, and she had not asked
what, and she had not warned anyone,

132
00:11:31,163 --> 00:11:36,554
and she had flown to Basel for the signing and stood
at the back of the delegation and

133
00:11:36,574 --> 00:11:40,455
waited to find out whether she was a
person who could do that.

134
00:11:41,024 --> 00:11:43,090
She found out at fourteen eighteen.

135
00:11:45,159 --> 00:11:50,915
He was waiting for her in the suite. He had not
changed his coat. It was on the back of

136
00:11:50,935 --> 00:11:54,367
a chair, torn at the shoulder,
still grey with dust.

137
00:11:56,057 --> 00:11:58,105
I didn't know what they were going to do.

138
00:11:58,625 --> 00:11:59,309
I know.

139
00:11:59,830 --> 00:12:01,730
I knew they were going to do something.

140
00:12:02,250 --> 00:12:03,360
I know that too.

141
00:12:05,880 --> 00:12:07,176
He turned around.

142
00:12:07,745 --> 00:12:13,517
The Cold-Read had been reading her for sixteen years.
He knew what she would do next before

143
00:12:13,537 --> 00:12:19,181
she did. She would tell him. All of it — the card in
the wallet, the phone from the mall,

144
00:12:19,201 --> 00:12:24,590
the twelve reports, the satellite call from a boat.
She had decided on the flight. He

145
00:12:24,610 --> 00:12:28,918
could see the decision sitting in her face
like a stone in a stream.

146
00:12:29,488 --> 00:12:30,832
He did not let her.

147
00:12:31,522 --> 00:12:32,697
Sit down, Catherine.

148
00:12:34,096 --> 00:12:38,927
You've been talking to Julian Ashcombe since May of
twenty thirty-three. You called him

149
00:12:38,947 --> 00:12:44,002
from a phone you bought at the Christiana mall. You've
sent him nine reports in four years.

150
00:12:44,022 --> 00:12:48,742
In the last one you told him the money was the
wrong picture, and he agreed with you,

151
00:12:48,762 --> 00:12:52,144
and that is why four people died in
that square this morning.

152
00:12:54,663 --> 00:12:56,179
How long have you known?

153
00:12:56,700 --> 00:13:02,143
Since the morning after you bought the phone. You
said good morning to me in the corridor

154
00:13:02,163 --> 00:13:05,275
on the eleventh floor and your
face was — relieved.

155
00:13:07,794 --> 00:13:09,587
Then why am I still alive?

156
00:13:12,609 --> 00:13:18,593
He handed her a glass of water. She did not take it.
He set it down on the table beside

157
00:13:18,613 --> 00:13:18,870
her.

158
00:13:19,559 --> 00:13:23,443
Because you are the only person left who
tells me when I am wrong.

159
00:13:25,344 --> 00:13:29,728
Evelyn is a zealot. Tomás is exhausted. Crane is in
love with me in a way that makes him

160
00:13:29,748 --> 00:13:34,033
useless. Wyatt and the quiet ones cannot disagree
with me — not will not, cannot, they

161
00:13:34,053 --> 00:13:38,438
are physically incapable of it, and there are more of
them every year. Pell and Hale and

162
00:13:38,458 --> 00:13:42,943
Gus have never once had an opinion. Dana is grateful.
The Territory is a hundred and sixty

163
00:13:42,963 --> 00:13:45,697
thousand people who would walk into
the sea if I asked.

164
00:13:46,096 --> 00:13:51,528
And you sat in a glass office in twenty twenty-eight
and told me you would know if I lied

165
00:13:51,548 --> 00:13:54,653
to you. You were right. You have
known. Every time.

166
00:13:57,173 --> 00:13:58,384
So you keep me.

167
00:13:58,903 --> 00:13:59,779
I keep you.

168
00:14:00,299 --> 00:14:01,858
And the channel to London?

169
00:14:02,379 --> 00:14:07,676
Stays open. You'll go on sending your reports. I'd
prefer you didn't tell Julian anything

170
00:14:07,696 --> 00:14:12,755
that gets people killed, but I won't stop you. I
would only have to find out what you

171
00:14:12,775 --> 00:14:17,954
were doing all over again, and that took four days
the first time, and I am busier now.

172
00:14:20,972 --> 00:14:23,153
She sat in the chair for a long time.

173
00:14:23,724 --> 00:14:29,314
She understood, sitting there, that she had not been
caught. She had been kept. That he

174
00:14:29,334 --> 00:14:35,053
had known for nine years, and had let her go on
believing that she was a spy in the house

175
00:14:35,073 --> 00:14:40,727
of a monster, because a spy who believes she is free
is more honest than a prisoner, and

176
00:14:40,747 --> 00:14:42,081
he needed her honest.

177
00:14:42,652 --> 00:14:48,597
She was fifty-one years old. She had been his chief
financial officer for fifteen years.

178
00:14:48,617 --> 00:14:54,495
She had a hundred and eighty million dollars, four
homes, and a seat at the table where

179
00:14:54,515 --> 00:14:55,987
the world was decided.

180
00:14:56,557 --> 00:15:02,566
And she understood, in a hotel suite in Basel, with
his torn coat on the back of a chair,

181
00:15:02,586 --> 00:15:08,460
that she was the most thoroughly owned human being
in his entire empire — because every

182
00:15:08,480 --> 00:15:14,354
single one of the others was owned by something that
had been done to them, and she had

183
00:15:14,374 --> 00:15:19,300
done it to herself, one reasonable decision
at a time, for fifteen years.

184
00:15:22,489 --> 00:15:23,887
I want to resign.

185
00:15:24,408 --> 00:15:25,302
No, you don't.

186
00:15:27,822 --> 00:15:31,686
She did not resign. Not that year, or
the next, or ever.
